<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Musa ibn Nusayr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">640–716</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Musa ibn Nusayr was the Umayyad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> who completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His subordinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a> crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in 711 and defeated the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>; Musa followed in 712 with a larger army and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 714, he fell from favour with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and died in obscurity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His ancestry is variously reported. Al-Tabari says his father was an Arab Christian of the Banu Yashkur held hostage after the fall of Ayn al-Tamr in 633, while al-Baladhuri calls him an Arab of the Balī tribe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. As a slave his father entered the service of Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, who freed him, and Musa was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with the date given as 640. The caliph Abd al-Malik made him co-governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. After a quarrel over missing tax money he was told to pay a huge fine or lose his head, and Abd al-Aziz paid the ransom and later had him appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 698 Musa became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, the first not subordinate to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and he completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. He did not impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by force but respected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> traditions and used diplomacy, and many Berbers converted and entered his army, possibly including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangiers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a>, and built a navy that conquered Ibiza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>.</p><p>Sources say he decided to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> when Julian, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, encouraged him with accounts of the injustice of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>. After a successful raid of 400 men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> he sent Tariq with about 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, who defeated Roderic's much larger army at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>. Musa then landed with 18,000 men, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> after a siege of three months and Mérida after five, and met Tariq at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> ordered him to report to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, he delayed in order to continue north, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> and reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> before he placed governors across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In Damascus Musa entered the city in spite of a request to delay, and brought the booty before the ailing al-Walid. After al-Walid's death his brother Sulayman stripped Musa of his rank and confiscated the spoils. His son Abd al-Aziz, who had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>'s widow Egilona, was assassinated, and another son, Abd Allah, was executed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s orders. Musa died naturally while on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> with Sulayman in about 715 or 716. Because of his disgrace, some medieval historians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> gave his deeds to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a>. Legend made him the hero of a tale of the city of brass, told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a>, and the Moroccan peak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Jebel Musa</a> is said to be named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Musa ibn Nusayr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His superior <b>Musa ibn Nusayr</b> followed in 712, and within a few years most of the peninsula had been conquered, often by treaties with local lords such as Theodemir of Tudmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/al-andalus-umayyad-province">Al-Andalus (Umayyad province)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Wadi al-Qura, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.54,35.1&amp;year=689" aria-label="Hebron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5455 1015 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Musa ibn Nusayr"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1285h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.54,35.1&amp;year=689">What was here in 689 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
